    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ecddd9d9-c087-550b-8cb8-544072051004",
      ":question/problem": "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2}+16z^{2}=64.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that the ellipse $x^{2}+16z^{2}=64$, or equivalently $\\frac{x^{2}}{64}+\\frac{z^{2}}{4}=1$, in the $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{64}+\\frac{z^2}{4}&=\\frac{(8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{64}+\\frac{(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{4}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,y)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y,\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $y\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is $8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
